(Ord. No. 2017-45, § 1, 6-6-17)

418:
(a) General provisions. Except as hereinafter provided, any change order or other modification of a contract term shall be approved by the board of commissioners.
(b) Statement of policy. Clayton County is committed to a policy of open, nondiscriminatory, and competitive purchasing. When circumstances arise after award of a contract, requiring modification of that contract, such modification will be accomplished in accordance with the procedures established by the director, to achieve the following goals:
(1) Ensure that Clayton County does not pay more than is necessary to complete the contract;
(2) Preclude a contractor from tendering the lowest bid and then increasing the cost of the contract through the change order process;
(3) Ensure that the terms and conditions upon which the contract was awarded are met throughout the term of the contract, including any and all change orders;
(4) Ensure that the change order procedure is not used to bypass the competitive bidding process; and
(5) Ensure that change orders are not used for work that is independent of and outside the scope of the original contract.
(c) Justification for change orders. Change orders are authorized only for the following reasons:
(1) Situations creating an immediate need to protect the public health, safety or welfare;
(2) Corrections of deficiencies in design or construction documents provided by architects or engineers other than the contractor;
(3) Changes in applicable laws or regulations, or changes that result from public participation when such participation is mandated by laws or regulations;
(4) Concealed conditions, differing site conditions or abnormal inclement weather;
(5) Owner requested changes within the scope of the original contract. Such changes may include: Deductive change orders and accommodations of value engineering and administrative matters such as closeout change orders for unit price contracts, deductions for approved material substitutions and administrative no-cost change orders.
(d) (1) Change order procedure. The ordinary sequence of a change order is as follows:
a. Need for contract modification is identified;
b. Contractor is requested to propose price for change and if necessary, schedule changes;
c. Contractor and county negotiate price and scope of change;
d. Agreement between county and contractor for change is clearly defined in a written modification;
e. Contractor signs modification and returns it to the [county]; and
f. Modification is submitted to the county manager or BOC for approval depending on the amount of the change order.
(2) Circumstances may alter this general description of the change order procedure. The administrative actions necessary to accomplish a change order are described in subsection (e) of this section.
(e) Administrative actions. Director of central services has the primary responsibility for completion of the administrative steps necessary to complete a change order. Such responsibility may be exercised through designees and in consultation with other interested departments.
(f) Authority of the chief operating officer. The chief operating officer or his/her designee shall have authority to approve all change orders to purchase orders and contracts up to an absolute value of ten percent of the original contract price, provided the total change order amount is less than $74,999.99. Change orders to any contract originally approved by the board of commissioners, chief operating officer or director of central services, which amend the scope of work, term, time and/or total cost not exceeding $74,999.99 may be approved by the chief operating officer or his/her designee. Change orders shall not be artificially divided so as to remain under the ten percent threshold.
(g) Authority of the director of central services. The director of central services and/or his/her designee shall have authority to approve all change orders to purchase orders and contracts up to an absolute value of $50,000.00. Change orders to any contract originally approved by the board of commissioners, chief operating officer or director of central services, which amend the scope of work, term, time and/or total cost not exceeding $50,000.00 may be approved by the director or his/his designee. Change orders shall not be artificially divided so as to remain under the $50,000.00 threshold.
(h) Ratification. All change orders shall be ratified by official action of the board of commissioners at the next board of commissioners' meeting as part of the monthly report from the director of central services and/or the chief operating officer.
(Ord. No. 2017-45, § 1, 6-6-17; Ord. No. 2019-12, § 1, 1-2-19; Ord. No. 2019-43, § 3, 4-2-19; Ord. No. 2020-119, § 6, 7-7-20; Ord. No. 2022-134, § 3, 6-21-22; Ord. No. 2022-147, § 1, 7-5-22)

